# Shell scripts MUST keep LF line endings, on every machine.
#
# This box runs `core.autocrlf=true`, so without this rule git hands the working
# copy CRLF and a later commit from a machine with a different autocrlf setting
# can store CRLF in the blob. A .sh checked out with CRLF fails on macOS/Linux
# with:
#     bash: ./script.sh: /usr/bin/env^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
# ...and a CRLF git hook silently stops firing. Both are miserable to diagnose
# because the file looks perfect in an editor.
#
# Added 2026-08-03 alongside `docs/migrations/scripts/restore-env-master.sh`,
# the macOS twin of the secrets restore — the first script here that is REQUIRED
# to run on a Mac. All 4 tracked .sh blobs were already LF at that point, so this
# rule renormalises nothing; it pins the current state.
#
# Deliberately NARROW. `* text=auto` would renormalise the whole tree in one
# commit across several parallel story lanes — do not add it without a reason.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Antigravity's machine-global cache MUST be a byte mirror of the doors, on every machine.
#
# `sync-agents.ps1` writes `~/.gemini/antigravity/global_workflows/*.md` with LF, but this box
# runs `core.autocrlf=true`, so git hands the working copy of `.agents/workflows/*.md` CRLF on
# every checkout. `test_command_surfaces.py --case CS-18` case L byte-compares door against
# cache, so on Windows it goes RED after any pull — 39 of 40 files "differ" while the content
# is byte-identical once the CRLFs are stripped. It could only be green in the window between
# a sync and the next git operation, which is not a state anyone can hold.
#
# Measured 2026-09-01 (SCC-338, PC pickup): door 756 bytes / 13 CRLF vs cache 743 bytes / 0 CRLF,
# identical after CRLF->LF. The Mac never sees it — nothing there is converting.
#
# Pinning the doors to LF makes the byte-mirror contract true on both machines rather than
# weakening CS-18 L to a normalised compare, which would stop it detecting a real drift.
.agents/workflows/*.md text eol=lf
